## 🔬 Layer-by-Layer Technical Breakdown

### Layer 1 — Ehlers Super Smoother Filter

| Property | Value |
|---|---|
| **Origin** | J.F. Ehlers, *Cybernetic Analysis for Stocks and Futures* (2004) |
| **Type** | 2-Pole Recursive Butterworth Filter |
| **Purpose** | Remove high-frequency noise with ~50% less lag than EMA |

**Why it matters:** Every moving average introduces lag. A 20-period SMA has 10 bars of lag. A 20-period EMA has ~6.5 bars. The Super Smoother achieves equivalent smoothing with only **~3 bars of lag** by using a 2-pole recursive filter design from electrical engineering.

**The math:**
```
arg = √2 × π / Period
a₁ = e^(-arg)
b₁ = 2 × a₁ × cos(arg)
c₂ = b₁,  c₃ = -a₁²,  c₁ = 1 - c₂ - c₃

Filter = c₁ × (Price + Price[1]) / 2 + c₂ × Filter[1] + c₃ × Filter[2]
```

> [!TIP]
> This is the same class of filters used in radar signal processing and audio engineering. It's the optimal way to remove noise from a 1D signal while preserving the underlying waveform shape.

